2025-09-05 00:38:05,003 - evaluation_logger_Cross-277 - INFO - Final Response: Great choice of base—Hotel Route‑Inn Sakaide‑Kita Inter sits right by the Seto-Chuo Expressway, so you can reach many of Kagawa’s best sights quickly. Here’s a concise, practical roundup of fun attractions in nearby cities, with typical travel time by car from your hotel and what to expect.

Sakaide (0–15 minutes)
- Seto Ohashi Memorial Park and Bridge Exhibition Center
  - What: Waterfront park beneath the Great Seto Bridge with lawns, views, and a small museum explaining the bridge’s engineering.
  - Why it’s fun: Iconic photo spots of the bridge; occasional night illuminations on select weekends/holidays.
  - Plan 1–2 hours; museum entry is modest; check hours (museum typically 9:00–17:00; closed some Mondays/holidays).
- Kagawa Prefectural Higashiyama Kaii Setouchi Art Museum
  - What: Works by celebrated painter Kaii Higashiyama, plus Seto Inland Sea views.
  - Why: Calm, scenic cultural stop next to the bridge park.
  - Plan ~45–60 minutes; typically closed Mondays.
- Goshikidai Highlands (viewpoints)
  - What: Ridge drive with panoramas of the Seto Inland Sea and islands.
  - Why: Sunset spot; easy pull-offs for photos.
  - Plan 45–90 minutes including short walks.

Utazu (10–15 minutes)
- Gold Tower (Utazu)
  - What: 158 m observation tower with views of the Great Seto Bridge and islands.
  - Why: Best single vista close to the hotel; night views are great.
  - Plan ~45–60 minutes; admission applies.
- Utazu Seaside (Rinkai) Park
  - What: Family-friendly waterfront park with promenades and play areas.
  - Why: Relaxed stroll, picnic, or sunset photos.
  - Plan 30–60 minutes.

Marugame (15–25 minutes)
- Marugame Castle (Kameyama)
  - What: One of Japan’s 12 original wooden castle keeps; dramatic stone walls; views over the city and Seto Inland Sea.
  - Why: Compact, authentic castle experience.
  - Plan 60–90 minutes; small admission for keep; grounds often free.
- MIMOCA (Marugame Genichiro‑Inokuma Museum of Contemporary Art)
  - What: Contemporary art museum designed by Taniguchi Yoshio (MoMA architect).
  - Why: Strong rotating exhibitions; right by Marugame Station.
  - Plan 60–90 minutes.
- New Reoma World (Ayauta area, ~30–40 minutes)
  - What: Large theme park with rides, seasonal illuminations, kids’ areas.
  - Why: Full family day out.
  - Plan half to full day; separate fees for entry and rides; check operating calendar.

Kotohira (25–35 minutes)
- Kotohira‑gu Shrine (Kompira‑san)
  - What: Famous shrine on Mt. Zozu with 785 steps to the main shrine (1368 to inner shrine).
  - Why: Classic pilgrimage challenge, wide views, lively shrine town with Sanuki udon shops.
  - Plan 2–3 hours; no main admission; go early to avoid heat/crowds.
- Kanamaru‑za (Konpira Grand Theatre)
  - What: Japan’s oldest surviving Kabuki playhouse; tours of stage and backstage.
  - Why: Unique look into traditional theatre; occasional performances.
  - Plan 45–60 minutes; modest entry; often closed on certain weekdays.
- Nakano Udon School (Kotohira)
  - What: Hands‑on Sanuki udon making and tasting.
  - Why: Fun, tasty, kid‑friendly.
  - Plan ~60–90 minutes; reservations recommended.

Zentsuji (20–30 minutes)
- Zentsu‑ji Temple
  - What: Birthplace of Kobo Daishi (Kukai), founder of Shingon Buddhism; key Shikoku Pilgrimage site.
  - Why: Historic temple precincts, treasure hall, and photogenic five‑story pagoda.
  - Plan 60–90 minutes; grounds largely free; small fee for museum/treasure hall.

Takamatsu (35–45 minutes)
- Ritsurin Garden
  - What: One of Japan’s finest daimyo gardens with ponds, teahouses, and borrowed‑scenery pines.
  - Why: Top must‑see; optional wasen (traditional boat) rides.
  - Plan 2–3 hours; small admission; seasonal early openings; boats extra and popular.
- Tamamo Park (Takamatsu Castle Ruins)
  - What: Seawater‑moat castle site with gardens and turrets.
  - Why: Unique maritime castle feel close to the port.
  - Plan 45–60 minutes; small admission to certain areas.
- Yashima Plateau + Shikoku Mura
  - What: Hilltop battlefield viewpoint over the Inland Sea; adjacent open‑air museum with historic rural buildings and vine bridge.
  - Why: Mix of nature, views, and architecture.
  - Plan 2–3 hours combined; admissions apply.

Mitoyo (45–60 minutes)
- Chichibugahama Beach
  - What: Flat tidal beach famous for “mirror” sunset photos on calm days at low tide.
  - Why: Iconic Instagram shot; timing matters (low tide + sunset).
  - Plan 60–90 minutes; check tide tables before you go.
- Shiude‑yama
  - What: Mountain lookout over island‑studded seas; cherry blossoms in spring.
  - Why: One of Kagawa’s best panoramic views.
  - Plan 60–90 minutes; short walk from parking.

Kanonji (55–70 minutes)
- Kotohiki Park and Zenigata Sunae (sand coin)
  - What: Giant sand sculpture of an Edo‑period coin on the beach.
  - Why: Quirky, unique photo stop with a hilltop viewpoint.
  - Plan 30–60 minutes.
- Takaya Shrine Otorii (Mt. Inazumi)
  - What: Dramatic mountaintop torii gate with sea backdrop.
  - Why: Spectacular photos; short hike from upper parking.
  - Plan ~60–90 minutes; narrow mountain road; wear good shoes.

Island day trips from nearby ports (via Takamatsu Port, ~40 minutes from hotel)
- Naoshima (art island)
  - Highlights: Chichu Art Museum (advance timed tickets essential), Benesse House Museum, Art House Project, Yayoi Kusama pumpkins.
  - Plan full day; frequent ferries from Takamatsu; rent bikes/bus on the island.
- Teshima
  - Highlights: Teshima Art Museum (timed tickets), Les Archives du Coeur, scattered installations.
  - Plan 4–6 hours; ferries from Takamatsu or Naoshima.
- Shodoshima
  - Highlights: Kankakei Gorge and Ropeway, Angel Road (appears at low tide), soy sauce brewery streets.
  - Easiest: Drive to Takamatsu Port, ferry to Tonosho; rent car/bus on island.
  - Guided option: 1‑Day Shodoshima Island Bus Tour from Tonosho Port is available from about US$59; convenient if you don’t want to drive.

Optional across the Great Seto Bridge (Okayama side, 60–80 minutes)
- Kurashiki Bikan Historical Quarter
  - What: Edo‑era canal district with white‑walled warehouses, boat rides, cafés, Ohara Museum of Art.
  - Why: One of the most charming old towns in western Japan.
  - Plan half‑day. Private guided half‑day tours are available if you prefer a structured visit.

Getting around tips
- Car: Easiest from your hotel; most spots above are 15–45 minutes away; parking is generally straightforward.
- Train: JR Sakaide Station connects west to Marugame/Zentsuji/Kotohira (change to JR Dosan Line) and east to Takamatsu. Good if you want to avoid driving in cities.
- Ferries: For Naoshima/Teshima/Shodoshima, Takamatsu Port has the most frequent services. Check ferry timetables and museum ticket availability before you go.
- Udon: Kagawa is Sanuki udon central—drop into local shops near any of these sights for an inexpensive, delicious meal.

